{:edb/db-before-t 1073 :edb/db-after-t 1074 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534386 50 #inst "2026-10-06T19:20:39.010Z" 13194139534386 true] [17592186100626 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186100626 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0$." 13194139534386 false] [17592186100626 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t$. First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2$:\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting\n\n$$\nt^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= t^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nt \\geq0\n$$\n\nand $x=2t^2$, we must have\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$" 13194139534386 false] [17592186100626 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186100627 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139534386 false] [17592186100627 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186100627 1145 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186100627 1145 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x = \\csc   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\csc   t = x \\\\ y = 2 \\cot   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\cot   t = \\frac{y}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1\n$$\n\nby $\\sin^{2}{t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n1 + (\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\sin t})^{2} \\\\\n1 + \\cot^{2} t &= \\csc^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and\n\n$$\n\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\ninto this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{y}{2})^{2} &= x^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4\\left(x^2-1 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $x=\\csc t$, the interval on the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139534386 false] [17592186100802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285376 13194139534386 true] [17592186100802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285380 13194139534386 true] [17592186100802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285383 13194139534386 true] [17592186100802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285388 13194139534386 true] [17592186100803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285355 13194139534386 true] [17592186100803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285362 13194139534386 true] [17592186100803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285369 13194139534386 true] [17592186101123 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534386 true] [17592186101123 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186101123 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$x = \\frac{1}{t - 2}, y = t^{2}, t \\ne 2$." 13194139534386 false] [17592186101123 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186101124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534386 true] [17592186101124 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2 \\sin   t - 1 , \\, \\, \\, y = 5 \\cos   t + 4 , \\, t \\in  [ 0 , 2 \\pi  ) .$" 13194139534386 false] [17592186101124 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186101124 1145 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186101125 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534386 true] [17592186101125 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186101125 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2 \\cos   t , \\, \\, \\, y = 3 \\sin   t , \\, t \\in  [ 0 , 2 \\pi  ) .$" 13194139534386 false] [17592186101125 1145 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285356 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1089 "q-102682" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1113 #uuid "d7c181af-f10d-5053-8fb3-b6d77c28247a" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285355 1145 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285357 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285358 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285359 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285360 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285361 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1041 "selection" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285357 13194139534386 true] [17592186285356 1110 #uuid "cf047c30-261c-5c45-8660-2be9d0c04098" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285357 1034 "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534386 true] [17592186285357 1111 #uuid "66417d04-d38c-5e39-9e51-1e8879351c79" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285358 1034 "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" 13194139534386 true] [17592186285358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534386 true] [1